}}The '''2026 Hyndburn Borough Council election''' was held on 7 May 2026, to elect 11 of the 35 members of [[Hyndburn Borough Council]] in [[Lancashire]], England, being the usual approximate third of the council. This was on the same day as other [[2026 United Kingdom local elections|local elections across the country]].

Hyndburn requested the election to be cancelled,<ref>{{Cite news |date=2026-01-15 |title=Revealed: The councils that asked to cancel elections |url=https://www.lgcplus.com/politics/governance-and-structure/revealed-the-councils-that-asked-to-cancel-elections-16-01-2026/ |access-date=2026-01-16}}</ref> but the government abandoned its initial plans to cancel some 2026 elections following a legal challenge.<ref name="bbc16feb26"/>

Prior to this election year, the council was under [[Labour Party (UK)|Labour]] majority control. 

== Background ==
Before the election Labour had 21 councillors, the [[Conservative Party (UK)|Conservatives]] had 13 councillors and the [[Green Party of England and Wales|Greens]] had 1. Eighteen or more seats are needed for overall control of the council. Labour were running the council as a majority administration.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2024-05-03 |title=Labour wins Hyndburn and unseats opposition Tory leader in Chorley |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c72pv80xy24o |access-date=2025-10-01 |website=BBC News |language=en-GB}}</ref>

The seats up for election in 2026 had last been contested in 2022. Due to [[2024–present structural changes to local government in England|plans]] outlined by the Government in 2024 to abolish several borough councils in Lancashire in favour of new unitary authorities, it was not known if the 2026 election in Hyndburn would be required. However, in 2025 Labour council leader Munsif Dad claimed that the elections would be going ahead as planned.<ref>{{Cite news |date=2025-03-03 |title=Borough's local elections WILL go ahead in 2026 councillors told |url=https://www.lancashiretelegraph.co.uk/news/24973080.hyndburn-local-elections-will-go-ahead-2026-councillors-told/ |access-date=2025-10-01 |work=Lancashire Telegraph |language=en-GB |issn=0307-1235}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=2025-02-27 |title=Agenda item - Announcements |url=https://www.hyndburnbc.gov.uk/working-at-hyndburn-borough-council/ |access-date=2025-10-01 |language=en-GB}}</ref>

Despite initially confirming that the elections would take place as planned, Labour council leader Munsif Dad later wrote to the government to request a delay to 2027, and the government announced its plans to delay the election.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2025-11-14 |title=Call to delay 2026 local elections in Lancashire over devolution |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cwypev4qp29o |access-date=2026-01-22 |website=BBC News |language=en-GB}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=2026-01-14 |title=Transitioning Lancashire councils seek to postpone elections |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cdxj5d282dno |access-date=2026-01-22 |website=BBC News |language=en-GB}}</ref> On 16 February 2026, the government abandoned its plans to delay the elections after a legal challenge and confirmed that they will take place as originally planned.<ref name="bbc16feb26">{{cite web|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c70ne31d884o|date=16 February 2026|title=Government abandons plans to delay 30 council elections|website=BBC News}}</ref>

As a result of the Elections Act 2022 electors were required to present photographic identification to polling staff in order to cast their vote. Postal voters were not affected.

